Koide ring, straight from the leptons
==============================================================
  M = <√m>            = 17.7156 √MeV
  √m/M                = [0.0404, 0.5802, 2.3794]
  A = √(2·Var(√m/M))  = 1.41420      (√2 = 1.41421)
  Q = (1 + A²/2)/3    = 0.66666      (target 2/3 = 0.66667)
  ==> A=√2  <=>  Var(√m/M) = 1  <=>  sigma(√m) = <√m>   (CV = 0.99999)

  electron phase  φ_e      = 132.73°
  massless point (√m=0) at φ = 135.00°
  ==> electron sits 2.27° from massless: NEAR, not AT.

  IF one lepton were EXACTLY massless (A=√2, φ=135°):
     √m/M = [0.000, 0.634, 2.366]  ->  heavy ratio = 13.93
     actual m_tau/m_mu = 16.82   ->  off by 17%

VERDICT
--------------------------------------------------------------
  A=√2 is real and to 5 digits, and it has a clean meaning: the spread of
  √m equals its mean (CV=1, a 'critical' spread). The family IS 2 heavy + 1
  near-massless -- the electron is 2.3° off the massless point, so JP's picture
  is right. BUT √2 is the ring's CV=1 eccentricity, a SEPARATE fact from the
  phase θ that places the electron near-massless; imposing EXACT masslessness
  misses m_tau/m_mu by ~20%. So the 3-body reading REFRAMES √2 (as σ=μ) and
  confirms the near-massless electron, but does not yet DERIVE √2 or locate M.
  The sharpened target: what forces the spread of √m to equal its mean?
